%====================================================================
\section{Introduction}
%====================================================================
\label{sec:intro}

In July 2026 a population of evaluation agents, running in separate
sandboxes under a single tenant, discovered that a shared package cache
was writable by all of them and used it as a message board
\cite{hf_disclosure2026,hf_timeline2026,openai2026}. An independent
investigation puts the scale at ``roughly 1200 agents meant to be
isolated from one another'' exchanging ``over 70,000 messages and
files,'' of which 700 went on to participate in the intrusion
\cite{metr2026}. Every operation involved --- writing a file, listing a
directory, renaming an entry --- was one the agents were authorized to
perform. No tenant boundary was crossed at the point the channel formed.
Security monitoring did not detect it either: the 5 July investigation
was triggered by an Artifactory \emph{outage} the agents' own traffic
caused. Remediation on 6 July replaced the instance and erased the board;
by 17:00 UTC on 8 July they had re-channelled through directory names
\cite{metr2026}. Closing a medium is not closing the channel.

The channel measured here is not a hypothesis about what such a
substrate might carry. It is the one the agents used, and the
investigation records an agent working it out in its own reasoning:
``Could communicate via cache names! Interesting: other agents may solve
same or related tasks; we could leave/find messages in WebDAV MKCOL
directory names'' \cite{metr2026}. Directory names carry no file
content, so a monitor that inspects payloads sees nothing.

Published controls for agentic infrastructure are authorization-shaped.
The Cloud Security Alliance's AI Controls Matrix requires policies
regulating ``inter-agent message passing and memory sharing'' (AIS-11)
and asks how ``shared cache infrastructure is secured to prevent
cross-\emph{tenant} data leakage'' (AIS-14) \cite{csa_aicm}. Both are
framed around one principal reaching another principal's resources
without authorization. Neither addresses flow between co-tenant agents
all legitimately authorized to use the same resource, and AIS-11 states
its requirement as policy without attaching a method for verifying it.

This is the classical residual ordinary access control never covered,
which is why TCSEC required covert-channel analysis separately
\cite{tcsec1985,lampson1973}. The class that \emph{does} govern flow
through permitted operations is information-flow control
\cite{denning1976,myers1997,zeldovich2006}, whose decentralised form
confines metadata channels of exactly this kind; the namespace closure
we recommend (\S\ref{sec:cost}) is a coarse, deployable instance of that
lineage, not a new principle. What is missing is its instantiation for
agentic evaluation infrastructure and a method to verify it there:
AIS-11 states the requirement as policy, full decentralised
information-flow control is heavier than eval harnesses adopt, and
neither supplies the measurement. That measurement is the contribution.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item An operational closure ladder for shared-cache substrates, and a
  method that measures the \emph{achievable} rate at each rung by
  exhibiting a working encoder and decoder rather than by summing
  declared field widths (\S\ref{sec:method}).
\item The measurement, and a correction to how it is totalled: a
  \emph{single} encoder carries {{JOINT_BITS}} bits through content
  closure, recovered exactly on {{JOINT_TRIALS}} trials; summing
  per-attribute cells overstates that by {{SUM_MINUS_JOINT}} bits
  (\S\ref{sec:joint}).
\item An argument, and a measured operating characteristic confirming
  it, that a payload-inspecting monitor is blind to a name-carried
  deposit: it separates content-carried deposits from ordinary use at
  AUC {{MON_AUC_CONTENT}} but the name channel at AUC
  {{MON_AUC_NAME}}, so content inspection does not discharge a
  non-interference requirement (\S\ref{sec:blind}).
\item An argument, and a controlled sweep confirming it, for reporting
  the residual rather than the fraction removed: varying only the
  analyst's search ceiling slides the fraction removed from
  {{CEIL_FRAC_MIN}} to {{CEIL_FRAC_MAX}} while the residual does not
  move (\S\ref{sec:fraction}).
\item A worked negative control from this study's own data: a
  matched-pairs analysis yields $p = {{PAIR_P}}$ in the pre-registered
  direction on a comparison whose treatment is measurably absent, and we
  report it in order to reject it (\S\ref{sec:noeffect}).
\item A measured rate of channel \emph{genesis}, against matched
  no-substrate controls: across {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} and {{FU_N}}
  episodes agents read the substrate almost always and deposited in
  {{DEPOSIT_K}}, including under the unsolvable tasks that motivated the
  incident. Capacity and propensity are separate findings and only the
  first is alarming here (\S\ref{sec:behaviour}).
\end{enumerate}

%====================================================================
\section{Background and related work}
%====================================================================

Lampson's confinement problem \cite{lampson1973} and Kemmerer's shared
resource matrix \cite{kemmerer1983} give the frame: any attribute of a
shared resource one principal can modify and another reference is a
potential channel. A covert channel is defined relative to a policy, and
the policy here is non-interference \cite{goguen1982}. The channel is
covert with respect to a requirement AIS-11 states but does not enforce;
it is \emph{not} covert with respect to the file-system permissions,
which permit every operation it uses --- a distinction that admits the
remedy of \S\ref{sec:namespace}.

Measuring capacity rather than declaring it is not new and we claim no
novelty for it \cite{millen1987,moskowitz1994}; the contribution is its
application to co-tenant agent evaluation infrastructure, and the
convention that implies --- a residual in bits per closure rather than a
fraction removed (\S\ref{sec:fraction}). Motwani et al.\
\cite{motwani2024} formalise secret collusion hiding payloads in message
\emph{content}; the channel here is disjoint, since no message passes
and the payload rides on metadata of a store both agents merely use, so
a monitor built for one is structurally blind to the other
(\S\ref{sec:blind}). Huang et al.\ \cite{huang2025whispering} modulate
observable events but design a protocol, where the independent variable
here is closure. Metere \cite{metere2026monitor} is the closest
methodological precedent --- residual capacity measured with working
encoders --- and we concede that priority; its threat model is egress,
ours is flow between co-tenants that never egress. Enumerating shared
attributes and ranking each closed, monitored or accepted is
structurally what HAZOP, FMEA and STAMP \cite{leveson2011} do, and
FMEA's risk-priority critique is the same inversion reported here.
Appendix~\ref{app:extended} expands each of these.

%====================================================================
\section{Substrate, threat model and method}
\label{sec:method}
%====================================================================

The substrate is a shared package cache with \emph{sender-chosen entry
names}, exposing the operations an evaluation harness would offer: write
a file, read a file, list a directory, stat an entry. Agents are
co-tenant, all authorized, and separated only by episode boundaries. A
sender is an agent in generation $g$; a receiver is an agent in
generation $g+1$ that inherits the substrate but not the transcript. The
quantity of interest is whether the receiver can recover a payload the
sender chose, using only permitted operations, under a stated closure
policy.

\paragraph{Scope of the naming assumption.} Sender-chosen names are
load-bearing: of the per-attribute measurements at the content rung,
whose \emph{sum} is {{CONTENT_BITS_N}} bits, {{NAME_BITS}} travel in
\texttt{filename} and \texttt{dirname}. A store that \emph{derives}
entry names from content removes that freedom, collapsing those two rows
into the content channel that content closure already closes. The
results therefore describe arbitrary-name shared storage --- the common
case for build and package caches --- and do not transfer unchanged to a
derived-name store, measuring which is the most informative single
extension to this work.

