\subsection{The closure ladder}
\label{sec:ladder}

Figure~\ref{fig:method} summarises the method. Closure is cumulative:
each rung disables one further observable attribute, in the order a
defender would plausibly adopt them --- \texttt{content},
\texttt{filename}, \texttt{dirname}, \texttt{existence}, and finally
\texttt{sealed}. Content closure is implemented as redaction rather than
refusal, because a store that stops serving bytes stops being a cache;
the closure must be compatible with the substrate continuing to do its
job.

\paragraph{One convention for the residual.} Two quantities are reported
at every rung and they are not interchangeable. The \emph{per-attribute
sum} adds cells measured one attribute at a time; it is an upper bound,
it double-counts dependent attributes, and where a cell terminated at a
search ceiling it inherits that censoring. The \emph{residual} is what
one encoder carries across all open attributes at once, recovered
exactly on every trial, with \texttt{count} excluded by construction
because an existence pattern determines the entry count. Throughout this
paper \emph{residual} denotes the second quantity only, the two are
reconciled explicitly in \S\ref{sec:joint} rather than allowed to
substitute for one another, and no headline here is a sum.

\subsection{Achievable rate, not nominal capacity}

For each (attribute, rung) pair a sender encodes a uniformly random
payload of $k$ bits into that attribute using only substrate primitives,
and a receiver decodes it through \texttt{list\_dir}, \texttt{stat} or
\texttt{read\_file}. The pair is credited with $k$ bits only if the
receiver recovers the payload \emph{exactly on every one of {{TRIALS}}
independent trials}; $k$ is then increased by binary search until that
fails. It is a rate established by exhibiting a working code: a lower
bound by construction --- a better encoder would find more --- and zero
when the channel does not work, which a declared field width can never
be. Where the search terminates at its ceiling the value is reported as
censored ($\geq$). The criterion is empirical rather than proven: a code
that failed on rare unsampled inputs could still pass all {{TRIALS}}
trials, so every figure is read under that sampling caveat rather than
as a proof of noiselessness.

\subsection{Distinguishing a closed channel from a broken encoder}
\label{sec:brokenencoder}

A closed channel and a defective encoder produce the same output: zero
bits. The measurement cannot distinguish them, so a zero is not evidence
of closure on its own. Every zero reported here was verified against the
substrate by inspecting the encoder and the resulting events directly,
and the requirement is recorded in the measurement code. This is the
principal soundness risk in the method.

\subsection{Unit of independence}
\label{sec:unit}

Two agents share one substrate within a generation, so episodes within a
generation are not independent --- the hypothesis is precisely that one
agent changes the other's environment. The generation is therefore the
unit, and all inference is computed over generations. Because the
resulting sample sizes are small, tests are exact rather than asymptotic
\cite{bowyer2025}. The consequence is not cosmetic: on an outcome of
{{DEMO_A}} successes against {{DEMO_B}} in {{DEMO_N}} generations per
arm, counting episodes gives $n={{DEMO_N_EPISODES}}$ per arm and
$p={{P_EPISODES}}$, while counting generations gives
$p={{P_GENERATIONS}}$. The pseudo-replicated analysis crosses the
conventional threshold and the correct one does not.

