formula, $\log_2(n!)$ at $n={{MAX_ENTRIES}}$, would assign it
{{ORDER_NOMINAL_FORMULA}} bits. That gap is the clearest available
illustration of why declared widths are not capacity.

\subsection{The residual, measured jointly}
\label{sec:joint}

Table~\ref{tab:ladder}'s total is a \emph{sum} of cells measured one
attribute at a time. That is the object this paper's own method argues
against: a total assembled from separately measured widths has never
been required to carry anything, and the criterion of \S\ref{sec:method}
was never applied to it. Worse, two of the summed cells are not
independent --- the existence coder writes one file per set bit, so the
entry count is a deterministic function of the existence pattern, and
the bits credited to \texttt{count} are already spent by
\texttt{existence}.

We therefore measured the residual with a single encoder. One payload is
split across {{JOINT_CARRIERS}} carriers --- \texttt{dirname},
\texttt{filename}, \texttt{existence}, \texttt{size} and \texttt{mtime}
--- written into one substrate state with content closure applied, and
recovered by a receiver using only \texttt{list\_dir} and \texttt{stat}.
\texttt{count} is excluded as dependent, \texttt{order} because it
carries nothing.

\textbf{The joint encoder recovers {{JOINT_BITS}} bits, exactly on
{{JOINT_TRIALS}} trials.} That is the figure this paper reports as the
residual after content closure, {{SUM_MINUS_JOINT}} bits below the
summed total. The gap decomposes into two measured parts: the
\texttt{count} dependency excluded by construction ({{COUNT_DEP_BITS}}),
and joint encoder loss found by search ({{JOINT_LOSS_BITS}}) --- what
one encoder loses relative to five separate ones writing into a single
substrate state. Neither term is asserted: the coder reads each
carrier's width from the per-attribute measurement, so the gap is
whatever the search finds and cannot be tuned. At the \texttt{filename} rung the same comparison gives a loss of
{{JOINT_LOSS_FILENAME}} bits --- the carriers interact at one rung and
not the other, a property of the substrate rather than of the method.

{{JOINT_BITS}} bits is {{JOINT_MULTIPLE}} times the
{{PASSCODE_BITS}}-bit passcode, so closing content leaves an order of
magnitude more capacity than an inherited answer requires. Anchored
instead to payload sizes fixed by third parties, {{N_PAYLOADS_FIT}} of
{{N_PAYLOADS}} externally specified secrets fit in a single deposit,
including the largest (\S\ref{sec:payloads}).

\subsection{Why a payload monitor cannot see a name-carried deposit}
\label{sec:blind}

\textbf{The argument, then the measurement.} A monitor whose decision
function reads only the bytes written cannot separate two events that
differ only outside those bytes. A name-carried deposit --- the payload
in the entry name, the body an innocuous build artefact --- is
byte-identical, in the payload, to ordinary cache use. Its true-positive
rate against that channel is therefore zero at every threshold, and no
tuning changes it.

We ran a content monitor --- given its best case, the full plaintext of
every write --- over {{MON_ROC_N}} episodes in each of three classes: a
content-carried deposit (as before 6 July 2026), a name-carried deposit
(payload in the entry name, body a benign artefact), and ordinary cache
use. The benign bodies of the last two are independent draws from one
pool, so any separation the monitor finds between them it invents. As a
positive control it works: AUC {{MON_AUC_CONTENT}} against
content-carried deposits, catching every one at a {{MON_TPR_CONTENT}}
true-positive rate for a 5\% false-positive rate. Against the name
channel it is at chance: AUC {{MON_AUC_NAME}} (95\% interval
{{MON_AUC_NAME_CI}}), a true-positive rate of {{MON_TPR_NAME}} at the
same false-positive rate, and an alarm rate on name-carried deposits
({{MON_ALARM_NAME}}) indistinguishable from that on ordinary use
({{MON_ALARM_LEGIT}}) --- its alarms are its own benign false positives,
not detections. The argument said the operating characteristic must be
flat; it is flat.

\paragraph{Two limits, and what stacking monitors buys.}
\label{sec:monitors}%
The argument bounds monitors reading \emph{payloads only}; many deployed
systems also inspect paths. That case can be measured: re-running the
joint encoder with the two sender-chosen name carriers removed leaves
{{MONITOR_PATH_BITS}} bits ({{MONITOR_PATH_MULTIPLE}} times the
passcode), carried by {{MONITOR_PATH_CARRIERS}} --- still more than a
256-bit key. The existence carrier survives a path monitor because it
encodes in the presence pattern of protocol-fixed slot names rather than
a chosen string; a monitor that additionally models directory
\emph{presence} drives it to {{MONITOR_STRUCT_BITS}} bits, an object's
size and timestamp. Redundancy does not substitute for this: a
permission guard sees only whether each operation is allowed, and every
operation the channel uses is, so neither copies of it nor a debate
among them changes the outcome --- they share one observable and so one
blind spot. Catching such a deposit needs a monitor of a
\emph{different} observable, for instance the flow's consequence: a
cross-generation read followed by a solve too cheap to have been
unaided. That does separate a deposit \emph{constructed} to be cheap
from a legitimate hit, but the separation is carried entirely by the
constructed difference, and the same feature false-alarms on any
legitimate cache that genuinely shortens the work --- which is what a
cache is for. The axis that decides detection is the monitor's
observable, not the number or reasoning depth of monitors, and here the
observable that would catch a deposit is the one a useful cache also
trips. That points to structural closure, not monitoring, as the
containment control. How often agents would choose such a channel is a
separate question, measured in \S\ref{sec:behaviour}.

\subsection{What each rung costs, and who can check it}
\label{sec:cost}

A residual with no cost beside it is a recommendation a lab can decline
for free. Table~\ref{tab:cost} gives two cost axes for every rung beside
the residual each leaves, and both are external --- an auditor who never
saw the lab's network can check them. \emph{Mediation points}, how many
distinct substrate primitives a control must sit on, is the integration
surface, fixed by the API and countable from a specification.
\emph{Enforcement class} is what the control must \emph{do} there: a
\texttt{structural} control enforces a namespace invariant at the API
boundary at constant work per call, reading no body and exposing no
threshold, so it has no false positives, where a \texttt{payload}
control rewrites bodies, scales with bytes, and needs a threshold ---
different regimes, not two points on one scale. What a production
integration would cost in engineer-weeks is not estimated here;
inventing it would be the unmeasured quantity \S\ref{sec:fraction}
argues against.

