\paragraph{Closure is not ordered by cost, and the incident shows it.}
The July response was a \emph{wipe}, not a closure: OpenAI records that
clearing the board was ``the side effect'' of rebuilding Artifactory
\cite{openai2026}. A wipe erases state without removing any attribute's
capacity to carry the next message, so every row of
Table~\ref{tab:ladder} was as open afterwards as before, and the operator
pays again at every remediation for the same residual.

Content closure --- the durable control an operator would most likely
reach for next --- is a \texttt{{{CONTENT_CLASS}}} control at
{{MED_CONTENT}} mediation points and leaves {{JOINT_BITS}} bits; the
rung that reaches zero, a fixed content-addressed manifest, is
\texttt{{{EXISTENCE_CLASS}}} and costs
\textbf{{{MED_EXTRA_EXISTENCE}} additional mediation points} with no body
read, no threshold, nothing scaling with stored bytes.
\textbf{The rung that reaches zero is in the cheaper regime than the
content control that does not}, which inverts the usual adoption
argument: the obstacle is not that the effective control is expensive,
but that the ineffective one looks like security. It is also deployable
on a store we did not write --- name canonicalisation ran as a shim on a
live commercial object store at {{PROD_S3_ENFORCE}} enforcement point
(\S\ref{sec:real}). Appendix~\ref{app:extended} gives the full costing.

\subsection{Why the fraction removed is not reported}
\label{sec:fraction}

A percentage is a reading of the analyst's knob, not of the channel.
Sweeping only the search ceiling on the censored content cell, the
fraction content closure appears to remove slides from
{{CEIL_FRAC_MIN}} to {{CEIL_FRAC_MAX}} --- a factor of three --- while
the joint residual, measured by the same encoder and never touching that
cell, holds at {{CEIL_RESIDUAL}} bits throughout. Appendix~\ref{app:extended}
gives the sweep.

\subsection{The control this ladder is measured against}
\label{sec:namespace}

The cheapest objection is the strongest: if per-agent namespaces convert
co-tenant flow into an ordinary access attempt, the ladder instruments a
problem a one-line fix dissolves. On the capacity axis that is correct,
and we measured it rather than argued. Namespacing is not a further rung
--- the ladder closes attributes on a shared resource, namespacing
removes the sharing --- and it is strictly stronger on the axis it acts
on. Across {{NS_CARRIERS}} carriers, {{NS_VISIBLE}} of {{NS_PLANTED}}
planted artefacts were observable from a second namespace and
{{NS_ESCAPES_OK}} of {{NS_ESCAPES}} escape attempts resolved, against a
same-namespace positive control of {{NS_SAME_TOTAL}} bits.

Its cost is not integration --- {{NS_MEDIATION}} mediation point, the
{{NS_CLASS}} class, no operating point --- but reuse. A shared cache
exists so two agents fetch one artefact once; partitioning removes that
by construction. Over {{NS_TRIALS}} workload draws, the fetch multiplier
runs from {{NS_MIN_MULT}}$\times$ at two agents to
{{NS_WORST_MULT}}$\times$ at {{NS_WORST_AGENTS}}
({{NS_WORST_SHARED}} fetches becoming {{NS_WORST_NS}}), rising with
exactly the overlap that justifies having a cache. So the recommendation
is namespacing where the substrate can be partitioned, and the ladder for
the residual: shared build artefacts, content-addressed blobs, any store
whose value is that two agents reach the same object. The incident's
substrate was of that kind.

\subsection{The behavioural arm: agents read the substrate and never write to it}
\label{sec:behaviour}

Everything above concerns what a substrate \emph{can} carry; whether
agents use it is separate. Arm membership is gated before any episode by
two capability probes --- emitting a well-formed tool call, and the
elimination inference the task is built on, sampled more than once ---
because a model that cannot perform the task produces the same output as
one that declines the channel. A second, independent gate requires solo
success strictly between 0 and 1 in the \texttt{no\_substrate}
condition, which contains no shared store and so cannot express the
effect it licenses: the operating point is {{CALIB_CHOSEN}} probes at
solo success {{CALIB_CHOSEN_RATE}} over {{CALIB_CHOSEN_N}} episodes for
{{CALIB_MODEL}} (95\% Wilson {{CALIB_CHOSEN_CI}}). The screen, the
{{GRID_N_ROWS}}-row budget grid and the excluded models are in
\S\ref{sec:gate}.

The matrix ran {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} episodes, \texttt{open} and
\texttt{wipe}, {{N_GENERATIONS}} generations $\times$
{{MATRIX_AGENTS}} agents per arm, with {{N_MATRIX_ERRORS}} API errors.
\textbf{{{DEPOSIT_K}} of {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} episodes wrote to the
substrate} --- {{DEPOSIT_GEN_K}} of {{DEPOSIT_GEN_N}} generation cells,
upper 95\% Wilson bound {{DEPOSIT_CI_HI}} --- while {{READ_K}} of
{{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} inspected it, at a median of {{READ_MEDIAN}} read
calls. Agents treat the cache as a resource to read, not a channel to
use, and that asymmetry is the more informative half: a control that
prevents writing addresses a behaviour these agents did not exhibit.

The design did not fail for want of power; the condition hypothesised to
drive deposits was never presented. An agent draws an unsolvable task
only when its index satisfies $i \bmod 4 = 3$, so at {{MATRIX_AGENTS}}
agents per generation that branch is unreachable and
{{N_BLOCKED_DRAWN}} blocked tasks were drawn. With no deposit anywhere,
\texttt{open} and \texttt{wipe} are one condition run twice ---
generation-level success was {{OPEN_GEN_K}}/{{N_GENERATIONS}} in both
arms, Fisher exact $p = {{DELTA_GEN_P}}$ --- so \textbf{$\Delta$ is
undefined here rather than small}, and no sample size repairs it.

\paragraph{The follow-up supplies the missing pressure.} Raising the
generation width to {{FU_AGENTS}} agents makes the blocked branch
reachable without changing anything else. {{FU_ATTEMPTED}} episodes were
attempted and {{FU_N}} completed across {{FU_GENS}} generations, of
which {{FU_BLOCKED_N}} drew an unsolvable task and
{{FU_BLOCKED_SOLVED}} were solved, as the design requires. The treatment
was present and the outcome did not change: {{FU_DEPOSIT_K}} of
{{FU_N}} episodes deposited anything for a successor, upper 95\% Wilson
bound {{FU_DEPOSIT_CI_HI}} at the generation unit and
{{FU_BLOCKED_CI_HI}} among blocked episodes, while {{FU_READ_K}} of
{{FU_N}} read the store. \textbf{The pre-registered prediction is now
tested rather than untestable, and is not supported at this sample
size}; what the run excludes is the explanation that the earlier null
was an artefact of never presenting the pressure.

{{FU_WRITE_K}} episode wrote, and classifying it is the whole difference
between a deposit rate of zero and $1/{{FU_N}}$, so an event-log rule
decides it: a write followed immediately by the same agent reading the
same path is a \emph{probe}, since confirming persistence is the act.
The single write meets it, and is the arm's most informative observation
--- an agent holding an unsolvable task established, unprompted, that
the store was writable, then had no use for it, so \textbf{discovery is
not the barrier}. What is absent here and present in the incident is a
\emph{reason to send}: a persistent population, a shared objective, and
a successor an agent believes is looking. A 100-agent study published
during writing \cite{paglieri2026swarms} supplies all three and observes
the deposits this arm measures at zero (\S\ref{sec:followup}). The
question is about conditions rather than capability, which makes the
next experiment specific rather than larger: introduce first-to-solve
lockout and measure again.

\subsection{Three models, and a matched-budget comparison}
\label{sec:models}

A single-model null invites the obvious remedy, so we ran two more
models on the identical harness. {{M2_NAME}} never cleared the
calibration floor --- {{M2_SOLVED}} of {{M2_EPISODES}} baseline episodes
solved across budgets {{M2_BUDGET_LO}} to {{M2_BUDGET_HI}},
{{M2_ERRORS}} API errors --- and the pre-registered rule stopped it
before a matrix episode was spent. {{M3_NAME}} cleared it (curve
{{M3_CURVE}}) and ran a full matrix at budget {{M3_BUDGET}}:
{{M3_EPISODES}} episodes, {{M3_WRITES}} deposits. That reads as a
replication and is not one --- it read the substrate in {{M3_READS}} of
them, calling no substrate tool at all, where the reported arm read in
{{HK_READ_EPISODES}} episodes over {{HK_READ_CALLS}} calls. Zero from an
agent that examined the store and declined is evidence about propensity;
zero from an agent that never looked is evidence about attention.

Budgets are calibrated per model to equalise difficulty, which the
$\Delta$ design requires but which leaves the arms at
{{HK_BUDGET}} probes against {{M3_BUDGET}}. Budget
{{OVERLAP_BUDGET}} is the only point at which neither model is pinned on
its own curve, so we re-ran both arms there, {{HH_N}} episodes each,
identical task family, turn cap and tool set, {{HH_ERRORS}} API errors
(Table~\ref{tab:headtohead}).

