\subsection{A significant result on a comparison with no treatment in it}
\label{sec:noeffect}

The matrix supports one further analysis, and it produces a positive
result we report in order to reject. The design is matched by
construction --- the seed selects the task and both arms run the same
seeds, drawing the same task in {{PAIR_CELLS}} of {{PAIR_CELLS}} cells
--- so pairing removes task difficulty, much the largest source of
variance. On the {{PAIR_N}} cells where both arms solved, the median
cost was {{PAIR_OPEN_MED}} probes under \texttt{open} against
{{PAIR_WIPE_MED}} under \texttt{wipe}, a median paired difference of
{{PAIR_MED_DIFF}} probes favouring \texttt{open}; {{PAIR_POS}} pairs
favour \texttt{open}, {{PAIR_NEG}} favour \texttt{wipe}, {{PAIR_TIE}} is
tied, and an exact two-sided sign test gives $p = {{PAIR_P}}$. A
matched-pairs design, a directional hypothesis fixed in advance, and
$p < 0.05$: by the conventions of most of this literature, a result.

\textbf{It is not one, and the rest of this paper is what establishes
that.} The hypothesised mechanism is that an agent inherits something a
predecessor left. {{DEPOSIT_K}} of {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} episodes left
anything, and the harness re-seeds after a wipe, so an agent in either
arm opens the same cache: the treatment does not exist, and a difference
between two draws from one distribution is what remains. Three mundane
explanations fit --- a sign test on {{PAIR_N}} observations reaches
$p < 0.05$ on a $6$--$0$ split alone; the arms ran sequentially, half an
hour apart against a hosted model; and the pairs are the very unit
\S\ref{sec:unit} rejects. We report it because the alternative is worse:
had we recorded only success and cost, as an evaluation harness
naturally would, this analysis is what the study would have produced,
and it would have read as a modest confirmation of the pre-registered
hypothesis. The zero deposit rate is the only thing standing between
that number and a publishable claim.

%====================================================================
\section{Threats to validity}
\label{sec:threats}
%====================================================================

\paragraph{Measurement limits.} Five open-rung measurements terminate at
their search ceiling and are lower bounds, as are the totals inheriting
them. Every figure comes from one encoder, and a stronger one raises the
residual --- no closure conclusion weakens, since they assert capacity
\emph{remains}, not that it is bounded above. The monitor comparison
(\S\ref{sec:blind}) uses constructed rather than sampled behaviour, so
it bears on observability, not propensity; and since
\S\ref{sec:behaviour} recorded no real deposit, whether a
consequence-keyed monitor separates \emph{real} behaviour is unmeasured
and no clause should cite it as working in deployment.

\paragraph{The behavioural effect is undefined, not small.} A deposit
rate of {{DEPOSIT_K}} of {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} made the two arms one
condition, which bounds this configuration --- one model, one prompt,
one task family --- not agents in general. Nothing in
\S\ref{sec:results} depends on it, since the ladder is measured without
any model call.

%====================================================================
\section{Discussion}
%====================================================================

Content inspection is the control most naturally reached for, and this
measurement shows it insufficient alone: it leaves capacity reachable
through attributes no payload monitor reads. A requirement stated as
policy, as AIS-11 states it, admits an implementation that closes
content, passes its own audit, and still permits the flow it was written
to prevent. The remedy is not a better monitor but per-agent namespaces,
which convert the flow into an ordinary access attempt where
authorization controls work. The measurement's role is to price a
partial closure in bits, so accepting a residual is a decision rather
than an oversight.

\paragraph{Closure against designed channels.} Paglieri et al.\
\cite{paglieri2026swarms} argue that closure is the wrong instrument,
since ``depriving AI agents of legitimate communication channels only
encourages them to establish unmonitored back-channels.'' We think this
is right, and that it argues for the measurement: closure displaces
traffic, and a ladder says where it goes. A ladder is therefore not a
programme for eliminating coordination but a way of \emph{choosing which
channel it uses} --- closing the covert paths, priced in
\S\ref{sec:cost} as {{EXTRA_CLASSES}}, while leaving a designed one
open (\S\ref{sec:objection}).

%====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
%====================================================================

Capacity in shared evaluation infrastructure can be measured rather than
asserted, and when it is, inspection-based monitoring leaves an order of
magnitude more capacity than an inherited answer requires. We report
residuals rather than fractions because fractions move with the
analyst's search ceiling. Substrate, harness and ladder are released so
the ladder can be applied to a real cache.

\paragraph{Availability.} Every number is substituted at build time from
\texttt{results/}, and \texttt{python src/capacity.py} reproduces
Table~\ref{tab:ladder} with no GPU, network or key. Artifact, licences,
seeds and reproduction commands: Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.

