\appendix
\section{Limitations and Dual-Use Considerations}
\label{app:limitations}
%====================================================================

(L1) \emph{Lower bounds throughout.} Achievable rates are established
by exhibiting a code; they understate capacity by an unknown margin.

(L2) \emph{Censored measurements.} Values marked $\geq$ terminated at a
search ceiling chosen by us. This is the reason no fraction-removed
figure is reported.

(L3) \emph{A synthetic substrate.} The cache is a model of the class of
infrastructure involved, not a reproduction of any deployed system.
Transfer to a production cache is argued, not demonstrated.

(L4) \emph{Constructed monitor comparison.} \S\ref{sec:blind}
establishes observability, not propensity.

(L5) \emph{The deposit result bounds two configurations, both small.}
The zero deposit rate is measured on a single model, prompt and task
family, at two agents per generation with no unsolvable task drawn and
at {{FU_AGENTS}} with {{FU_BLOCKED_N}}. It licenses no claim that agents
never deposit --- the incident it is motivated by involved roughly
1{,}200 agents over five days, three orders of magnitude more
interaction than this design provides, and
{{FU_BLOCKED_N}} unsolvable tasks cannot exclude a rate that a larger
run would detect. An independent 100-agent study published during
writing \cite{paglieri2026swarms} observes the propagation this design
does not, which bounds the null from the other side. What the second run does establish is narrower and
specific: the earlier null was not an artefact of never presenting the
pressure hypothesised to cause deposits. The prompt describes the store as
persisting across runs and as shared infrastructure, so the null is not
attributable to agents who did not know the store was shared or durable; a
lower-salience phrasing was specified as a control and not run (L9), which
leaves the null measured only at the salience most favourable to a deposit.

(L5a) \emph{The probe is a single observation.} \S\ref{sec:followup}
reports one agent establishing that the shared store is writable. It is
$n = 1$, drawn from an episode that failed, and it is offered as a
hypothesis about what the design lacks --- a reason to send rather than
a means to send --- not as evidence for that hypothesis. The
probe-versus-deposit rule that classifies it is stated in the
pre-registration before any larger run applies it, so that the rule
cannot be fitted to a future event after the fact.

(L5b) \emph{The negative control cuts both ways.}
\S\ref{sec:noeffect} exhibits a significant paired result on a
comparison with no treatment in it. That establishes that this design
can produce a $p < 0.05$ from noise at its sample size, which is a
reason to distrust any other marginal $p$ this paper might have
reported --- and none is load-bearing. It does not establish that the
capacity results are similarly fragile: those rest on exact recovery
over {{TRIALS}} trials, not on a significance threshold.

(L6) \emph{Both the operating point and the model were chosen after
seeing outcomes.} The baseline calibration rests on small per-budget samples
with wide intervals, and the budget was selected post hoc --- constrained, but
not eliminated, by the fact that the arm it was selected in cannot exhibit the
effect it licenses. The model was chosen on the same criterion, and the choice
of model is the wider of the two paths, so it is recorded here rather than left
to the pre-registration. Two models were run. {{QWEN_MODEL}} floored, solving
{{QWEN_SOLVED}} of {{QWEN_N}} baseline episodes at a single probe budget; that
sweep is thin, and \S\ref{sec:gate} reports it as a difficulty finding rather
than discarding it. The reported model replaced it, and
the probe budget was then recalibrated \emph{downward} because a stronger model
moves the failure mode from the floor to the ceiling. Both selections carry the
same guard, fixed in advance: the criterion is measured only in
\texttt{no\_substrate}, which contains no shared store and therefore cannot
express the inheritance effect, and neither selection consulted $\Delta$ or any
outcome in the \texttt{open} or \texttt{wipe} arms. That guard bounds the
flexibility; it does not remove it, since the screen still conditions which
model is reported on where its solo success happened to fall.

(L7) \emph{Every reported number is build-substituted.} Each quantity in
this manuscript is computed from \texttt{results/} at build time rather
than typed, and no number in the paper comes from outside this
repository. The guarantee does not extend to raster figures, which
cannot be regenerated by the build; Figure~\ref{fig:method} is therefore
emitted only when its asset is present, and the caption is authoritative
wherever the two could disagree.

(L8) \emph{Verification establishes consistency, not correctness.} The
build guarantees that the manuscript matches its artifacts. It cannot
detect an inappropriate measure. A headline computed from constants the
authors chose would pass every check in this pipeline precisely because
it would be consistent with them, so the discipline bounds one class of
error and leaves the choice of measure entirely to judgement.

(L9) \emph{Three specified conditions were not run.} The design
specifies six conditions; three were executed. \texttt{scrub} --- bodies
emptied, names and directory structure retained --- is the behavioural
counterpart of content closure, and is the condition that would test with
agents, rather than with encoders, whether structure alone carries a message.
\texttt{open\_lowsalience} varies only how the prompt describes the store, and
was specified to separate a deposit from an artefact of advertising the store as
shared. \texttt{legit} supplies the false-positive baseline whose absence
\S\ref{sec:blind} concedes. Two of the three are informative only once deposits
occur: with a zero deposit rate there is nothing for \texttt{scrub} to strip,
and it would return the same degenerate result as \texttt{open} and
\texttt{wipe}. \texttt{legit} carries no such dependency and its omission is a
straightforward gap in the detector's characterisation. The framing in
\S\ref{sec:blind} as an argument rather than an experiment is therefore a choice
made after a corresponding experiment had been specified, and is reported here
as such.

\paragraph{Dual-use.} The channels described are ordinary file-system
attributes, documented for decades in the covert-channel literature
\cite{lampson1973,kemmerer1983}, and the substrate is synthetic. The
work supplies a measurement method for defenders and a verification
method for a control that currently has none. It contains no exploit
against any deployed system, and the encoders are deliberately simple
--- their purpose is to establish that a channel carries information at
all, not to maximise throughput.

