\subsection{The same questions asked of real infrastructure}
\label{sec:real}

Everything above is measured on a substrate we wrote, and L3 concedes that
its parameters are ours. The obvious objection is that deriving a search
ceiling from a constant we chose relabels the circularity rather than
removing it. This section answers it by asking the same questions of a
real shared volume and a real artifact store, both in containers, with
the limits \emph{discovered} rather than declared.

\paragraph{A real shared volume.} Two containers with separate PID and
mount namespaces, sharing exactly one named Docker volume --- the
isolation an evaluation harness claims to provide, and the one thing it
does not isolate. Probing that mount through ordinary file operations:

