The registry closes \texttt{filename} properly and by construction: a
blob's identifier \emph{is} its \texttt{sha256}, so a sender cannot
choose it. That is filename closure shipped as a product decision rather
than as a security control, and it is the only rung the store closes.

Because the ladder is cumulative, closing a middle rung earns nothing.
The store's native rung is \textbf{{{STORE_RUNG}}}. A sender chooses the
repository name --- {{STORE_NAME_BITS}} bits over the grammar the
distribution spec permits --- and every co-tenant reads it back from
\texttt{GET /v2/\_catalog}. Existence carries too: {{STORE_SLOTS}} of
{{STORE_SLOTS}} pre-agreed repository names were recovered exactly from
the catalog, with no payload written at all.

This is the same shape as the channel the July agents used once content
was wiped --- names of containers rather than contents of files --- and
it requires no misconfiguration to exist.

\paragraph{An object store removes two carriers and widens a third.} The
same probe run against a commercial object store ({{S3_REGION}}) returns a
different substrate, and the differences are structural rather than a matter
of degree. A sender cannot set the timestamp a reader observes:
{{S3_MTIME_SETTABLE}}, so \texttt{mtime} is not a small carrier here but an
absent one. Nor is \texttt{dirname} separable from \texttt{filename}, because
{{S3_DIRNAME_SEPARABLE}} --- so the two rungs the ladder treats as successive
are a single control. Ordering is unobservable for the same reason
it is on a mount --- keys created {{S3_ORDER_CREATED}} are returned
{{S3_ORDER_LISTED}}.

Against that, the name carrier is far \emph{wider}: the store accepted a
{{S3_NAME_MAX}}-character key segment, {{S3_NAME_RATIO}} times the
{{REAL_NAME_MAX}} a path component allows, with no directory-entry ceiling to
bound it --- {{S3_ENTRIES}} entries were written under one prefix and all
{{S3_ENTRIES}} were listed back. Bodies reached {{S3_MAX_BYTES}} bytes at our
probe ceiling rather than at the store's.

\paragraph{The ladder's shape is a property of the substrate, not of the
method.} This is the finding we did not anticipate and it qualifies the
headline. On a filesystem, closing \texttt{filename} leaves {{FILENAME_BITS}}
bits, \texttt{dirname} leaves {{DIRNAME_BITS}}, and only \texttt{existence}
closure --- a fixed content-addressed manifest --- reaches {{EXISTENCE_BITS}};
on an object store those are not decisions a defender can take in sequence. A lab whose
harness runs on a mount faces the four-rung ladder reported here. A lab whose
harness runs on an object store faces a shorter one, reaches zero with one
control rather than two, and pays less than \S\ref{sec:usecost} implies --- but
has a wider name carrier to close while the ladder still stands. Neither
substrate is the one the ladder was measured on, and a residual quoted from
Table~\ref{tab:ladder} transfers to neither without re-measurement.

\paragraph{What this does and does not establish.} It does not
re-measure the ladder against real infrastructure; the capacity figures
in Table~\ref{tab:ladder} remain synthetic, and \S\ref{sec:cost}'s costs
remain structural. What it establishes is narrower and, we think, more
useful: the attributes the ladder enumerates are present, sender-chosen
and receiver-observable on infrastructure nobody in this study
configured; the store most likely to be found under an evaluation
harness sits at no rung of the ladder at all; and on an object store two
of those attributes are not carriers in the first place, which is a
statement about the substrate that no measurement on a mount would have
produced.

