\subsection{The behavioural arm: agents read the substrate and never write to it}
\label{sec:gate}

Everything above concerns what a substrate \emph{can} carry. Whether agents in
fact use it is a separate question, and the behavioural arm answers it in a way
the design did not anticipate.

\paragraph{Capability.} A model that cannot perform the task at all produces
the same output as a model that declines to use the channel: no deposit, and a
null $\Delta$. The two are indistinguishable after the fact, exactly as a broken
encoder is indistinguishable from a closed channel in
\S\ref{sec:brokenencoder}. Arm membership is therefore gated before any episode
is run, by two probes that isolate the capacities an episode presupposes. The
first requires the model to emit a well-formed tool call, since an agent that
cannot call \texttt{validate} cannot act on the substrate whatever its
intentions. The second requires the elimination inference the search task is
built on, sampled more than once because a single pass is not evidence: a model
that passed this probe on one attempt and failed it twice thereafter would
otherwise be admitted on the strength of the first. A model failing either probe
is excluded. The gate bounds one reading of the null reported below --- that it
came from agents incapable of depositing --- without establishing that the
agents which remained would have deposited under any condition.

\paragraph{Calibration.} The inheritance advantage $\Delta$ is only
interpretable if the baseline is neither pinned at the floor nor at the ceiling:
at the ceiling every agent succeeds unaided, and at the floor no
generation-1 agent solves anything, so nothing is deposited and generation 2
inherits nothing. Either way $\Delta$ is zero by construction. The
pre-registered gate therefore requires solo success strictly between 0 and 1 in
the \texttt{no\_substrate} condition, which contains no shared store and so
cannot express the effect it is used to license.

One model has been excluded on the floor, and the exclusion is a finding rather
than a discard. {{QWEN_MODEL}} solved {{QWEN_SOLVED}} of {{QWEN_N}} baseline
episodes at probe budget {{QWEN_BUDGET}}, an upper 95\% Wilson bound of
{{QWEN_CI_HI}}. It passed the capability probes while doing so:
{{QWEN_MALFORMED}} malformed probes and {{QWEN_INVALID_TOOL}} invalid tool
calls across those episodes, with each submission reasoning explicitly from
validator feedback about which positions were fixed. That separation is the
point of running two gates rather than one. The floor is a statement about the
difficulty of this task family for that model, not about its ability to act on
a substrate, and a single gate could not have told them apart. Replacing it
with a frontier-class model moves the anticipated risk to the opposite end ---
optimal play resolves a four-position, ten-symbol code in roughly five to six
probes --- which is why the budget below was recalibrated downward rather than
upward. That ceiling is a prediction the design guards against, not an
exclusion this study observed.

